#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48adeec5-fd51-5fd0-9cbb-865947abcfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432346} {:answer/id #uuid "603d35dd-8efa-5544-b92b-f29e859ccbb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432348} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fee454-4dd2-565f-bb9f-e0034cd35e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432356} {:answer/id #uuid "c0760b03-d85d-5f8d-87d7-29831c845c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432358} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bba0dc-736f-564b-a952-443458426144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0760b03-d85d-5f8d-87d7-29831c845c02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432358} :answer-field/id #uuid "d001a817-7196-53c1-ab12-d39da924742e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d7c182a-d8d9-5d67-83bd-5c70deb44da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162947" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}y+x{y}^{3}+{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & ∗ \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3} & {x}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy & 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & 6xy+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-1)+3+(x-1)=2x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0889e045-221d-548d-8586-c263b14daf17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278673} {:answer/id #uuid "36bda793-84bc-523f-874b-ecf8461dfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279071} {:answer/id #uuid "b804339b-81d0-5458-8b89-1119bfccd852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280210} {:answer/id #uuid "bec80d1d-dc4a-5247-8229-4f3da550f0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280269} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd330d2-e165-5873-970a-d719dc56d528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36bda793-84bc-523f-874b-ecf8461dfe2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279071} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd3902cd-4ea0-53f7-8e46-8bbcf944e194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0b608b5-0740-5c5c-a8d8-286478d0cfd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163143" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+y}+2x & \\frac{1}{x+y}+3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+2 & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+6y\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+4+(y-1)+(y-1)+5+(y-1)=3y+7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08639b07-25fa-583e-a83f-d337dffebecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281761} {:answer/id #uuid "152ed58b-832e-5b30-8ecd-f7c2f04c36a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281887} {:answer/id #uuid "4577ccab-91a5-5a0a-ad8e-553e88e2a326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282277} {:answer/id #uuid "84d9d89d-783c-5c61-8e59-033da0638e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282835} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6113b6-87b4-5fbe-ade6-6692083e0867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4577ccab-91a5-5a0a-ad8e-553e88e2a326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282277} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23126f6-69b8-59e0-8873-787033890983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0ff3e7e-07e5-5c92-aabb-370f248ce704" :question/math-academy-id "q-164611" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =3, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & ≈3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(4) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30badc79-fc5e-5344-ad7c-8a9a4154d658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282124} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6b341-3aec-58bc-922c-d954e7bbad7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282457} {:answer/id #uuid "5c368add-a07b-5dd9-b643-d26d0eafbba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282470} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b77b3e-3ac4-5c5d-9953-5b9f183b497e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283396} {:answer/id #uuid "c871d9c5-8ad7-58fa-aa05-e4c911b10055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30badc79-fc5e-5344-ad7c-8a9a4154d658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282124} :answer-field/id #uuid "e583c6d9-dc1d-5b2d-9463-46e1326bfe44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e72817fe-f751-5c2a-9faa-b82f2365cc69" :question/math-academy-id "q-164828" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & ≈-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-e+2e+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}e \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =e+\\frac{1}{2}(-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b5cfc5-5b79-5869-9762-17001e7c7019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432342} {:answer/id #uuid "41ed0217-d425-5f4e-ba99-e0ac900da7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432343} {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432345} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9aead5-698e-55fd-a7ed-c0810fdc4083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432355} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ec6153-7a5a-564c-b65e-6663c5889657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46fa0700-2e70-5cfa-8b00-54b4c7338732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432345} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ace458-f233-535c-b685-fcd05334410a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e65d6e32-4824-5250-9bff-fdfee93e977a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164860" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-1,-1) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(2,-1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(2,-1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =4, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1 \\\\ z+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & ≈4+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+\\frac{1}{2}(-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
